{
  "dom_id": "step-q143553",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both known, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${X}_{2}+{X}_{5}$\n\nII. $\\text{median}({X}_{2},{X}_{4},{X}_{6},\\mu )$\n\nIII. $\\text{mode}({X}_{1},{X}_{3},{X}_{5},{\\sigma }^{2})$",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression II is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ and the *known* population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression III is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ and the *known* variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
